---
import Figure from "@/components/Figure.astro";

type Link = {
  label: string;
  url?: string;
}

import emacsImage from "@/assets/emacs.jpg";

const petriEntries: Link[] = [
  {
    label: "Testing SCPI communication with an oscilloscope over a TCP socket in Clojure",
    url: "https://github.com/objectiveprover/Petri/blob/master/3-scpi-tcp-connection.clj",
  },
  {
    label: "Simple web template engine in TypeScript",
    url: "https://github.com/objectiveprover/Petri/tree/master/5-web-templating-engine_ts",
  },
  {
    label: "Executing commands at random intervals in Tcl",
    url: "https://github.com/objectiveprover/Petri/blob/master/1-random-wait.tcl",
  },
  {
    label: "Collection of “Hello, world” files in different languages",
    url: "https://github.com/objectiveprover/Petri/tree/master/0-hello-world",
  },
];


---

<section id="programming" class="page-section" aria-labelledby="programming-h">

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="prose col-8">
      <h2 id="programming-h">3—Programming</h2>
      <p>
        At the moment most of my programming is experimental, and the amount of public code is
          minimal because I’ve worked on building websites for clients most of my career. I’m looking to expand my skills in different languages, especially live ones (which
          can be modified while they run) like a Lisp or Forth, but also high-integrity ones like
          Ada/SPARK.
      </p>
      <p>For clients or employers, I welcome other more common programming languages like JavaScript/TypeScript, where I have the most experience in.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="page-subsection" aria-labelledby="emacs-h">
    <h3 id="emacs-h">
      3.1—The Minimalist Emacs
    </h3>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="col-6">
        <Figure src={emacsImage} alt="Emacs Lisp code in my Emacs configuration" ratio="8 / 5" class="framed" />
      </div>
      <div class="col-6">
        <p>
          An Emacs configuration for a clear reading experience and to allow higher focus on code semantics. It’s focused on reducing cognitive effort as much as possible, to meet my own needs.
        </p>
        <p>Contrary to common wisdom, a white background with dark monochrome text is easier to read than a dark background with various colors mixed in the text. It’s mostly unnoticeable by people with average-to-high cognitive capacity, though.</p>
        <p class="links"><a href="https://github.com/objectiveprover/.emacs.d">See on GitHub</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-subsection" aria-labelledby="petri-h">
    <h3 id="petri-h">3.2—Petri</h3>

    <div class="grid">
      <div class="col-6" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-m);">
        <p>
          Programs that don’t belong in a product or large project are stored in this repository, it works as a central place for testing ideas and learning/implementing algorithms.
        </p>
        <p class="links"><a href="https://github.com/objectiveprover/Petri">See on GitHub</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="col-6">
        <h4 class="list-heading">Some examples:</h4>
        <ol class="titles">
          {petriEntries.map((e) => <li class="entry"><a href={e.url}>{e.label}</a></li>)}
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</section>

<style>
.titles {
  margin-top: var(--space-m);
  padding-left: 1em;
  font-style: italic;

  a {
    text-decoration: none;
  }
}

.entry + .entry {
  margin-top: 0.5em;
}

.list-heading {
  font-size: var(--font-size-0);
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

</style>
